-- title:
--   Torus quotient of the finite part: multiplicative tower, Raynaud-exact
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (hypothesis `hHp`, expressed through `ZMod.unitsMap` for the divisibility $(M/p) \mid M$), with $M/p$ also nonzero. Fix a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $p$, in the sense that $p$ is a non-unit of $Pl$, whose residue field is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. Fix the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of the modular invariant lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, so that the two-chart integral models `toBase p Γ hj` over $R_p =$ [`ModularCurve.XHDRLevel.R p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L22) are available for $\Gamma =$ `ΓM M H` and $\Gamma =$ `ΓN p M H hpM`.
--
--   The geometric input consists of: an integral model datum $\mathfrak{X}$ of type [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L81); level data $\Lambda$ and a Néron object $O$ of type [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53), whose underlying scheme $O.G$ over the base `base p` carries a relative group law $O.L$ and a parametrisation `O.pts` of $J_H(M) =$ [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127), the degree-zero divisor class group of the base-changed function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`; and two representability hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ`, each asserting that the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero, tested on algebraically closed fields) is represented, for `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` with rigidification $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\infty}$ by the designation $(O.G, O.g)$ with zero section the unit section of $O.L$, and for `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` with rigidification the composite of $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\infty}$ with $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$ by the designation $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ with zero section the unit section of $\Lambda.L$.
--
--   The local ring data consists of a henselian local domain $R_h$ with an injective algebra map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that the image of $R_h$ lies in $Pl$ (`hRA`) and the maximal ideal of $R_h$ is exactly the set of elements whose image has $Pl$-valuation $< 1$ (`hRloc`), together with an $R_h$-algebra structure on $\mathbb{Z}/p$ whose kernel is described by the same valuation condition (`hres`).
--
--   The witness for the finite part consists of: a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R_h$ of height $h$ (a tower of finite free Hopf $R_h$-algebras `𝒢.level v` of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals); an additive map $\Delta$ from the group of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{G}$ to $J_H(M)$; and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between the Tate modules of $\mathcal{G}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and of $J_H(M)$. These are subject to: injectivity of $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`); the level identification `hΔlev`, that for each $v$ an element $y \in J_H(M)$ lies in the subgroup `O.finPts (p ^ v)` — generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes of the function field whose associated point of $O$ extends to the place — precisely when $y = \Delta(x)$ for some level-$v$ point $x$; Galois equivariance `hΔgal` of $\Delta$ for any pair consisting of $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and an $R_h$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing with it; the Hecke compatibility `hΔhecke`, that for every set of primes $S$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (a Hecke operator $T_\ell$, $U_q$ or a diamond $\langle d \rangle$) there is a levelwise family $\varphi$ of Hopf endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}$ commuting with the transition maps and inducing, through $\Delta$, the operator `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H(M)$; the description `he` of $e$ as $\Delta$ applied componentwise, together with injectivity `heinj` and the range description `herange`, that $y$ lies in the range of $e$ exactly when its $n$-th component lies in `O.finPts (p ^ n)` for all $n$; Galois equivariance `hegal` of $e$ relative to `𝒢.tateModuleRep` and [`ModularCurve.JH.tateGaloisRep`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L154); $p$-saturatedness `hsat` of the range of $e$; the hypothesis `hcoker` that the cokernel of $e$ is free of rank `O.toricRank` over $\mathbb{Z}_p$; and `htor`, that every element of `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` is of the form $\Delta(x)$ for a level-$v$ point $x$.
--
--   The Raynaud (toric) data consists of a second $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R_h$ of height $h_B$ together with levelwise Hopf algebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, a natural number $h'$, and the numerical hypotheses $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ (`hhB`) and $h_B = 2h'$ (`hhB2`). The maps $\psi$ satisfy: compatibility with the transition maps (`hψt`); the kernel criterion `hψker`, that the restriction of a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$ along $\psi_v$ is trivial if and only if $\Delta(x) \in$ `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`; surjectivity on points `hψsurj`, that every level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{B}$ arises by restriction along $\psi_v$ from a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$; the reduction criterion `hψred`, that if the restriction of $x$ along $\psi_v$ reduces to the identity point (all values differ from the counit image by elements of $Pl$-valuation $< 1$) then $x$ itself reduces to the identity point; and the period condition `hperiod`, that for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ of the degree-zero Picard group of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`, and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the restriction of $y$ along $\psi_v$ reduces to the identity point.
--
--   The compatibility of $\mathcal{G}$ with the Néron object is given by a ring homomorphism $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ compatible with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ subject to: `hιbase`, that $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ is the composite of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $R_h \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$; `hιcl`, that the induced lift into the pullback of $O.g$ along $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$ is a closed immersion; `hιp`, that $\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ for $O.L$ factors through the unit section, so that $\iota_v$ lands in the $p^v$-kernel; `hιpts`, that the point of $O$ attached by `O.pts` to $\Delta(x)$, for $x$ a level-$v$ point over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$; `hιmul`, that for every commutative $R_h$-algebra $B$ and every pair of level-$v$ points $x,y$ over $B$ whose associated morphisms lie over the base (hypotheses `hx`, `hy`), the morphism attached to $x \cdot y$ is the $O.L$-product of those attached to $x$ and $y$; `hιt`, that $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the transition map followed by $\iota_{v+1}$ equals $\iota_v$; `hιhecke`, that for every $S$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) there is a levelwise family $\varphi$ of Hopf endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}$ commuting with the transitions, intertwining $\iota_v$ with the Hecke correspondence `O.hecke S g`, and inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$; and `hιfin`, that for each $v$ the comparison morphism $j_v$ obtained by lifting $\iota_v$ into the pullback, over $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$, of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ (the pullback of multiplication by $p^v$ along the unit section) is both an open immersion and a closed immersion, and that every point of that pullback whose image under the projection to $\operatorname{Spec} R_h$ is the closed point lies in the image of $j_v$.
--
--   Finally, `hjB` requires that for each $v$ the base change $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes \psi_v$, formed as `Bialgebra.TensorProduct.map (BialgHom.id (ZMod p) (ZMod p)) (ψ v)`, is injective.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a family of types $T_v$ ($v \in \mathbb{N}$), each carrying a commutative ring structure, a Hopf algebra structure over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ which is cocommutative, and which is finite and free as a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-module; coalgebra-and-algebra maps $t^T_v : T_{v+1} \to T_v$; coalgebra-and-algebra maps $\pi^T_v : \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to T_v$; and bialgebra automorphisms $\mathrm{Ver}^T_v$ of $T_v$, such that all of the following hold: each $t^T_v$ is surjective; $\dim_{\mathbb{Z}/p} T_v = p^{v \cdot O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$; the kernel of $t^T_v$ is the $p^v$-torsion ideal [`PDivisibleGroup.Hopf.torsionIdeal (ZMod p) (T (v+1)) (p ^ v)`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L157); each $\pi^T_v$ is surjective; the square
--   $$\pi^T_v \circ (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{transition}_v) = t^T_v \circ \pi^T_{v+1}$$
--   commutes; for every $v$ and every character $\chi$ in the Cartier dual [`CartierDual (ZMod p) (T v)`](def/HopfAlgebra_CartierDual.html#L12), the Cartier dual of $\mathrm{Ver}^T_v$ sends $\chi$ to $\chi^p$; and the kernel of $\pi^T_v$, viewed as an algebra map, equals the ideal generated by the image under $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes \psi_v$ of the augmentation ideal (the kernel of the counit) of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$.
--
--   This is the special-fibre half of the Raynaud step in the analysis at $p$ of the Néron object of $J_H(M)$: the quotient of the $p$-divisible group of the finite part by the Raynaud subgroup $\mathcal{B}$ is realised, after reduction to $\mathbb{Z}/p$, as a tower of finite Hopf $\mathbb{F}_p$-algebras of rank $p^{v\,t}$ with $t$ the toric rank, multiplicative in the sense that Verschiebung is an isomorphism (its Cartier dual being the $p$-th power map), and exact against the Raynaud quotient. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_twoStepTower_raynaudQuotient_descent_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_twoStepTower_raynaudQuotient_descent_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins), which assembles the two-step tower feeding the level-lowering argument at $p$.
